INT. GRAND MERIDIAN HOTEL - SUBLEVEL LAUNDRY - NIGHT

Industrial washers thunder in rows. Steam curls along pipes. Carts of white linen stand in the aisles like silent ghosts.

CALLOWAY, fifties, a quiet man in a charcoal suit with a hotel badge that says SECURITY, leans on a dryer reading a folded newspaper. SABINE DUMAS, thirties, in a laundry uniform, loads sheets into a washer, never faster than necessary. REUBEN OKAFOR, twenties, a bellhop, sits on a laundry cart eating a sandwich. TANSY BRIGHT, forties, in a conference lanyard, hovers near the stairs.

He turns a page. She slams a washer door. Reuben chews.

                    TANSY
          Excuse me. I think I took a wrong turn.

                    SABINE
          Guests aren't allowed down here.

                    TANSY
          I know. I'm sorry. I was looking for the ice machine.

                    CALLOWAY
          Third floor.

                    TANSY
          I'm on the third floor.

Calloway folds his newspaper. Sabine finishes loading and stands straight. Reuben sets down his sandwich.

                    REUBEN
          Sabine, the thing from the Hartley party.

                    SABINE
          Not now.

                    REUBEN
          He'll want to know where

                    SABINE
          Reuben, not now.

He swings off the cart. Calloway takes a step toward Tansy, hands open, friendly.

                    CALLOWAY
          Ma'am, I can walk you up.

                    TANSY
          That's not necessary.

                    CALLOWAY
          I insist.

Tansy turns toward the stairs. Sabine moves to block her. The washers pound on.

                    TANSY
          Please move.

                    SABINE
          ^Please wait.

Two voices, one beat. Calloway sighs and slides the newspaper into his jacket.

                    CALLOWAY
          Tansy Bright. Conference B. You gave a talk on supply chain integrity this afternoon.

                    TANSY
          How did you

                    CALLOWAY
          We read the program.

She glances from him to Sabine to Reuben. Her hand finds the stair rail.

                    TANSY
          I'd like to leave now.

                    REUBEN
          Sure. We just want to ask one thing.

                    SABINE
          What did you see in the east elevator this afternoon?

                    TANSY
          Nothing.

                    CALLOWAY
          That's a very fast answer.

                    TANSY
          It's the true one.

                    REUBEN
          Ms. Bright, we're not the police.

                    TANSY
          That's what worries me.

Calloway nods slowly. He crosses to a washer and pulls the handle. The door swings open, and steam rolls out. Inside, among the sheets, a conference lanyard hangs from a hook.

                    SABINE
          Is that yours?

Tansy looks at her own lanyard. Then at the one in the machine. The numbers match.

                    TANSY
          I don't understand.

                    CALLOWAY
          Neither did the last one.

He closes the door. The washers keep running. Nobody moves.
